I wrote gay son in green.

1827
01:14:31.370 --> 01:14:32.110
Green means go.

1828
01:14:32.310 --> 01:14:34.350
So I know to go ahead and shut up about it.

1829
01:14:34.850 --> 01:14:35.130
Yes.

1830
01:14:35.210 --> 01:14:36.270
And orange means orange.

1831
01:14:36.390 --> 01:14:37.510
You glad you didn't bring it up.

1832
01:14:37.770 --> 01:14:39.390
Most colors mean don't say it.

1833
01:14:39.830 --> 01:14:40.070
Yeah.

1834
01:14:40.430 --> 01:14:41.570
But Dwight doesn't know that.

1835
01:14:42.290 --> 01:14:42.530
Yeah.

1836
01:14:42.690 --> 01:14:47.410
So Dwight is basically saying all the things that are color coded to not be said.

1837
01:14:47.610 --> 01:14:47.810
Right.

1838
01:14:47.810 --> 01:14:57.570
Well, before we wrap up this heavy competition between Mike, wait, this episode, that's the champagne.

1839
01:14:57.750 --> 01:14:58.070
I know.

1840
01:14:58.170 --> 01:15:01.290
And the champagne, you have a nickname for everyone in your champagne brain.

1841
01:15:01.390 --> 01:15:01.850
Mike.

1842
01:15:02.030 --> 01:15:02.410
Mike.

1843
01:15:02.790 --> 01:15:03.170
Dwighty.

1844
01:15:05.590 --> 01:15:06.270
Okay.

1845
01:15:07.410 --> 01:15:13.170
I think we should go back and wrap up this Andy Jim storyline.

1846
01:15:13.590 --> 01:15:13.810
Okay.

1847
01:15:14.010 --> 01:15:16.330
We both had to stop drinking champagne, by the way.

1848
01:15:16.850 --> 01:15:17.650
We did.

1849
01:15:18.030 --> 01:15:20.350
It just, it's so interesting.

1850
01:15:20.530 --> 01:15:22.490
Scotch and Splenda was like, woo-boo, woo-boo.

1851
01:15:22.650 --> 01:15:25.050
But champagne were like, I can't talk right now.

1852
01:15:25.290 --> 01:15:25.690
Yeah.

1853
01:15:25.810 --> 01:15:28.250
I still really do love the Scotch and Splenda.

1854
01:15:28.470 --> 01:15:28.890
Me too.

1855
01:15:28.890 --> 01:15:29.930
I have to say.

1856
01:15:29.930 --> 01:15:31.110
It's a really good drink.

1857
01:15:31.110 --> 01:15:36.530
And we got some letters from people who said it's basically an old fashioned if we'd added a few orange bitters.

1858
01:15:36.670 --> 01:15:38.150
And that's what it tasted like to me.

1859
01:15:38.250 --> 01:15:38.510
That's right.

1860
01:15:38.810 --> 01:15:40.770
Because I guess you would add like a sugar.

1861
01:15:41.050 --> 01:15:41.410
Yeah.

1862
01:15:41.770 --> 01:15:45.350
A muddled kind of sugary syrup to the Scotch.

1863
01:15:45.350 --> 01:15:45.530
Right.

1864
01:15:45.530 --> 01:15:47.550
And so that's what the Splenda is doing.

1865
01:15:48.030 --> 01:15:48.190
Yeah.

1866
01:15:48.610 --> 01:15:50.650
But champagne just makes us go, what?

1867
01:15:50.750 --> 01:15:51.550
What are we talking about?

1868
01:15:51.690 --> 01:15:52.030
What's happening?

1869
01:15:52.030 --> 01:15:52.410
I know.

1870
01:15:52.670 --> 01:15:52.850
Okay.

1871
01:15:53.330 --> 01:15:53.930
All right.

1872
01:15:54.970 --> 01:15:55.390
See?

1873
01:15:55.650 --> 01:15:56.170
I know.

1874
01:15:56.290 --> 01:15:57.430
I'm like, what am I talking about?

1875
01:15:57.610 --> 01:15:57.790
Okay.

1876
01:15:57.990 --> 01:15:59.130
So Andy and Jim.

1877
01:16:00.290 --> 01:16:03.550
Andy is going to tell the whole bullpen that Jim is upset.

1878
01:16:04.830 --> 01:16:09.810
And Phyllis is going to be like, Andy, I think Jim is just messing with you.

1879
01:16:10.050 --> 01:16:16.090
And then there's that smug shot of Jim in the kitchen looking out through the blinds with that grin on his face.

1880
01:16:16.090 --> 01:16:21.250
We had a fan question from Raya H and Poonet B.

1881
01:16:21.730 --> 01:16:25.170
Can we talk about how douchey Jim is being in this episode?

1882
01:16:25.710 --> 01:16:28.790
Andy was being really supportive and genuinely cared for Jim.

1883
01:16:28.950 --> 01:16:35.150
And while Andy's concern might have been misguided or nosy, I think it came from a good place.

1884
01:16:35.430 --> 01:16:43.890
We'd really like to get your take on this prank that Jim pulls on Andy because we think the prank might have been a little mean-spirited.

1885
01:16:44.550 --> 01:16:45.830
I didn't care for it.

1886
01:16:46.090 --> 01:16:46.810
I didn't either.

1887
01:16:46.930 --> 01:16:48.930
I do think this is a mean-spirited joke.

1888
01:16:49.110 --> 01:16:50.930
I mean, we already know I don't like pranks.

1889
01:16:51.130 --> 01:16:54.370
And I don't love especially pranks that-

1890
01:16:54.370 --> 01:16:55.310
At someone's expense.

1891
01:16:55.390 --> 01:16:56.750
That play with their emotions.

1892
01:16:56.990 --> 01:16:57.130
Right.

1893
01:16:57.330 --> 01:17:04.030
I think it was too big of a reaction to Andy's kind of nosy meddling.

1894
01:17:04.290 --> 01:17:07.490
And he does have this nice speech, Jim, at the end to Andy.

1895
01:17:07.530 --> 01:17:11.950
He's like, listen, I know Angela broke your heart, but you're going to find someone else.

1896
01:17:12.050 --> 01:17:12.850
You really will.

1897
01:17:13.310 --> 01:17:17.270
So I feel like that was the moment where the writers were like, you know, Jim's not a bad guy.

1898
01:17:17.490 --> 01:17:21.350
Maybe he was trying to show Andy something, but I just didn't like the prank.

1899
01:17:21.710 --> 01:17:22.390
Me either.

1900
01:17:22.670 --> 01:17:22.910
Yeah.

1901
01:17:23.210 --> 01:17:24.850
Well, I'm glad we discussed that.

1902
01:17:24.990 --> 01:17:25.110
Yeah.

1903
01:17:25.110 --> 01:17:25.750
We're with you.

1904
01:17:25.750 --> 01:17:29.310
So this episode ends with more cheeseball fun.

1905
01:17:29.810 --> 01:17:34.270
Michael, Ryan, and Pam, they're trying to see how many cheese puffs can they fit in their mouth.

1906
01:17:34.530 --> 01:17:34.910
32.

1907
01:17:35.290 --> 01:17:36.190
That's the record.

1908
01:17:36.350 --> 01:17:36.510
Yeah.

1909
01:17:36.510 --> 01:17:42.070
And then the phone rings and Michael answers it and he's got a mouthful of cheese puffs and we're all laughing.

1910
01:17:42.390 --> 01:17:48.630
And while this is happening, you can hear a Michael talking head where he's saying he wanted to start a company and not a war.

1911
01:17:49.290 --> 01:17:52.330
Well, this talking head was added later.

1912
01:17:52.610 --> 01:17:54.230
This was not in the original script.

1913
01:17:54.910 --> 01:17:58.650
Steve shot this a couple weeks after we shot the original episode.

1914
01:17:58.650 --> 01:18:02.970
And all of this footage of us doing this cheese puffs bit.

1915
01:18:03.110 --> 01:18:03.350
Yeah.

1916
01:18:03.510 --> 01:18:06.950
This was originally going to be at the end of the cold open.

1917
01:18:07.170 --> 01:18:09.810
So it was all of that was going to live at the top of the episode.

1918
01:18:10.030 --> 01:18:10.470
Yes.

1919
01:18:10.470 --> 01:18:16.510
But they thought it would be funnier to end the cold open on Pam getting the cheese puffs while she's yawning.

1920
01:18:17.110 --> 01:18:22.450
And then they gave this episode this new tag with the cheese ball bit.

1921
01:18:22.730 --> 01:18:25.090
This was not the original tag for the episode.

1922
01:18:25.090 --> 01:18:29.990
So instead of this bookend of cheese puffs at the beginning and end, there was a tag.

1923
01:18:30.530 --> 01:18:31.910
And we did film it.

1924
01:18:32.030 --> 01:18:33.430
And I want to share it with you guys.

1925
01:18:34.050 --> 01:18:38.110
So Andy is in the conference room and he is selling off his wedding stuff.

1926
01:18:38.270 --> 01:18:38.990
He's determined.

1927
01:18:39.790 --> 01:18:42.410
Well, you know, he didn't get buyers from Jim and Pam.

1928
01:18:42.630 --> 01:18:45.470
So in the tag, he's selling it to the rest of the gang.

1929
01:18:45.650 --> 01:18:47.390
And they're all bidding on things.

1930
01:18:47.810 --> 01:18:49.450
Stanley buys the rice bags.

1931
01:18:49.630 --> 01:18:52.030
And Creed decides to get in on the action, too.

1932
01:18:52.250 --> 01:18:53.930
I thought we had to hear a little bit of it.

1933
01:18:55.090 --> 01:18:56.270
Why are you here, Stanley?

1934
01:18:56.710 --> 01:18:57.970
I'm here for the rice.

1935
01:18:58.230 --> 01:18:58.530
Boom.

1936
01:18:58.750 --> 01:18:59.090
Sold.

1937
01:18:59.310 --> 01:19:00.090
100 bags of rice.

1938
01:19:00.190 --> 01:19:00.630
Stanley Hudson.

1939
01:19:00.930 --> 01:19:04.210
Creed, how would you like a crispy new tuxedo for your funeral?

1940
01:19:04.430 --> 01:19:05.670
I'll take the bottom half.

1941
01:19:07.030 --> 01:19:08.930
I want to go out the same way I came in.

1942
01:19:09.110 --> 01:19:09.550
Shirtless.

1943
01:19:10.930 --> 01:19:15.290
And everyone else starts making bids, too, asking questions on things.

1944
01:19:16.170 --> 01:19:20.690
Angela is not in the scene, but she walks by and had this talking head.

1945
01:19:20.690 --> 01:19:21.970
It was in the shooting draft.

1946
01:19:22.370 --> 01:19:24.090
I don't know why I'm down.

1947
01:19:24.350 --> 01:19:29.610
I guess I always thought that Andy would save the stuff from our wedding just in case.

1948
01:19:30.070 --> 01:19:31.890
No, it's stupid.

1949
01:19:33.110 --> 01:19:37.510
Just in case I married someone else and he decided to throw us a wedding.

1950
01:19:37.670 --> 01:19:38.730
I told you it was stupid.

1951
01:19:42.070 --> 01:19:44.690
I guess there were some things in there that Angela liked.

1952
01:19:45.010 --> 01:19:45.450
Yeah.

1953
01:19:45.590 --> 01:19:47.370
And maybe she hoped would make a comeback.

1954
01:19:48.450 --> 01:19:50.650
Ah, so there you have it, guys.

1955
01:19:51.010 --> 01:19:52.210
100 episodes.

1956
01:19:52.770 --> 01:19:53.670
Oh, my gosh.

1957
01:19:53.710 --> 01:19:55.410
We got it in just before the holidays.

1958
01:19:55.710 --> 01:19:55.990
Yeah.

1959
01:19:56.250 --> 01:19:58.450
I'm glad because that's always like a very crazy time.

1960
01:19:58.650 --> 01:19:59.270
It is.

1961
01:19:59.270 --> 01:20:02.250
It's the Halloween to New Year's.

1962
01:20:02.270 --> 01:20:04.390
It's a sprint to the end, I always say.

1963
01:20:05.130 --> 01:20:07.730
Well, listen, we're going to have a few breaks coming up.

1964
01:20:07.810 --> 01:20:09.870
We do take a break at Thanksgiving.

1965
01:20:09.870 --> 01:20:11.870
We take a break at Christmas and New Year's.

1966
01:20:11.950 --> 01:20:17.310
We will have some great reruns of episodes with fun little new stuff at the top.

1967
01:20:17.450 --> 01:20:17.590
Yes.

1968
01:20:17.590 --> 01:20:19.170
So be sure to listen to that stuff.

1969
01:20:19.650 --> 01:20:24.010
But listen, Ange, we got this letter because, you know, I said I was going through the mail.

1970
01:20:24.230 --> 01:20:24.370
Yeah.

1971
01:20:24.550 --> 01:20:25.710
From Susanna G.

1972
01:20:26.390 --> 01:20:27.850
And I wanted to share it.

1973
01:20:27.850 --> 01:20:29.430
I really appreciated it.

1974
01:20:29.570 --> 01:20:29.790
Okay.

1975
01:20:29.910 --> 01:20:30.470
Let's hear it.

1976
01:20:31.130 --> 01:20:35.990
Susanna says, not sure you'll see this, but I wanted to say something to you both.

1977
01:20:36.450 --> 01:20:38.230
First, you are my people.

1978
01:20:38.930 --> 01:20:44.770
You are silly, loud, honest, organized, Jenna, me too, and just so relatable.

1979
01:20:45.050 --> 01:20:46.510
She didn't put Angela in there for organized.

1980
01:20:46.510 --> 01:20:47.610
It's okay, Susanna.

1981
01:20:47.610 --> 01:20:55.390
But more importantly, I noticed your value of mental health mainly depicted to me by the

1982
01:20:55.390 --> 01:20:58.750
breaks that you take sometimes between podcast episodes.

1983
01:20:59.250 --> 01:21:01.090
Not all podcasts do that.

1984
01:21:01.230 --> 01:21:05.790
And as a fan, I always look forward to listening to next week's episode.

1985
01:21:05.790 --> 01:21:09.790
So when I hear rerun content, at times it's initially a bummer.

1986
01:21:09.790 --> 01:21:16.370
However, it's always followed by thoughts of, well, perhaps they needed more prep time so

1987
01:21:16.370 --> 01:21:19.590
that they didn't have to sacrifice family time or self-care.

1988
01:21:20.030 --> 01:21:25.530
And as a hustling mom with a career of my own, two small children, and a law enforcement

1989
01:21:25.530 --> 01:21:31.330
hubby with crazy schedules, demands, and stresses, my life is chaotic to say the leaSt.

1990
01:21:31.330 --> 01:21:37.350
But I'm learning more and more my stability and that of my family comes from not sacrificing

1991
01:21:37.350 --> 01:21:38.430
those two things.

1992
01:21:38.910 --> 01:21:44.310
So I commend you for staying committed to your values and still getting to do what you love

1993
01:21:44.310 --> 01:21:45.630
and with your bestie.

1994
01:21:45.870 --> 01:21:47.050
What a blessing.

1995
01:21:47.730 --> 01:21:50.850
Maybe I got all this wrong and that's not what's going on at all.

1996
01:21:50.970 --> 01:21:55.750
But if I'm on the nose, I simply want to offer encouragement to keep it up your way.

1997
01:21:56.190 --> 01:22:00.430
I'll be waiting for next week's episode whenever you get to it.

1998
01:22:00.430 --> 01:22:02.910
Sending virtual hugs to you both.

1999
01:22:03.230 --> 01:22:05.090
That is so sweet.

2000
01:22:05.650 --> 01:22:08.810
Susanna, you got it 100% right.

2001
01:22:08.970 --> 01:22:10.550
Yeah, I feel seen, Susanna.

2002
01:22:10.770 --> 01:22:17.290
Yes, it really meant a lot to me because I feel like Angela and I are two gals with a very

2003
01:22:17.290 --> 01:22:18.230
strong work ethic.

2004
01:22:18.450 --> 01:22:26.330
We love making this show, but it is also important to us that we reserve holiday time, a little

2005
01:22:26.330 --> 01:22:29.510
spring break time, a little summer time just for our families.

2006
01:22:29.510 --> 01:22:30.010
Yeah.

2007
01:22:30.390 --> 01:22:31.950
For our mental health breaks.

2008
01:22:32.130 --> 01:22:33.970
And so we do take time off for that.

2009
01:22:34.470 --> 01:22:36.890
You know, it also means that our team here gets a break.

2010
01:22:37.130 --> 01:22:37.530
That's right.

2011
01:22:37.670 --> 01:22:38.770
Everyone gets a break.

2012
01:22:39.110 --> 01:22:40.470
So thank you, Susanna.

2013
01:22:40.870 --> 01:22:41.970
I really appreciate it.

2014
01:22:42.110 --> 01:22:47.370
And just know that we will put some fun stuff at the top of those rerun episodes so that we

2015
01:22:47.370 --> 01:22:48.810
don't leave you hanging on those breaks.

2016
01:22:48.950 --> 01:22:49.510
That's right.

2017
01:22:49.710 --> 01:22:54.290
And you guys, I really want to say thank you so much to Creed Bratton as well for doing

2018
01:22:54.290 --> 01:22:56.210
that sweet little intro for us.

2019
01:22:56.410 --> 01:22:59.350
And you guys, he has a new single that just dropped.

2020
01:22:59.530 --> 01:23:00.830
It's called Breathe Easy.

2021
01:23:00.990 --> 01:23:02.570
It's available on Apple Music.

2022
01:23:02.650 --> 01:23:05.830
And I thought we could listen to a little bit of it as we say goodbye.

2023
01:23:06.150 --> 01:23:07.670
Happy 100th episode.

2024
01:23:07.930 --> 01:23:09.210
Happy 100th.

